**Proof.**

Let $V$ denote the (real) vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1999$. Then $\dim V = 2000 < \infty$.

Define $\|\cdot\|_1 : V \to \mathbb{R}$ by
$$\|p\|_1 = \int_{-1}^{1} |p(x)|\,dx.$$
We claim this is a norm on $V$. Nonnegativity and homogeneity are immediate. For definiteness: if $\|p\|_1 = 0$, then the continuous function $|p(x)|$ integrates to $0$ over $[-1,1]$, so $|p(x)| = 0$ for all $x \in [-1,1]$; a polynomial vanishing on an interval is identically zero. The triangle inequality follows from $|p+q| \leq |p|+|q|$. Hence $\|\cdot\|_1$ is a norm.

Now consider the evaluation map $L : V \to \mathbb{R}$, $L(p) = p(0)$. This is a linear functional on $V$.

**Key fact.** *On a finite-dimensional normed vector space, every linear functional is bounded.*

We prove this directly. Fix any basis $\{e_1, \dots, e_{2000}\}$ of $V$ and let $\|\cdot\|$ be any norm on $V$ (here $\|\cdot\| = \|\cdot\|_1$). Define the auxiliary function
$$\varphi(a_1,\dots,a_{2000}) = \left\|a_1 e_1 + \cdots + a_{2000} e_{2000}\right\|, \qquad (a_1,\dots,a_{2000}) \in \mathbb{R}^{2000}.$$
This is continuous on $\mathbb{R}^{2000}$ (it is Lipschitz: $|\varphi(a)-\varphi(b)| \leq \|(\sum (a_i-b_i)e_i)\| \leq \sum |a_i-b_i|\,\|e_i\|$). It is strictly positive on the compact set $S = \{(a_1,\dots,a_{2000}) : \sum a_i^2 = 1\}$, since $\varphi = 0$ on $S$ would mean a nontrivial linear combination of basis vectors is zero. Therefore
$$m := \min_{S} \varphi > 0.$$
By homogeneity, for every nonzero $v = \sum a_i e_i \in V$,
$$\|v\| = \left\|\sum a_i e_i\right\| = \left(\sum a_i^2\right)^{1/2} \varphi\!\left(\frac{a}{\|a\|_2}\right) \geq m\,\|a\|_2.$$
Equivalently, the coordinate map $v \mapsto (a_1,\dots,a_{2000})$ satisfies $\|a\|_2 \leq m^{-1}\|v\|$.

Now write $L(v) = \sum_{i=1}^{2000} a_i\, L(e_i)$ for $v = \sum a_i e_i$. By Cauchy–Schwarz,
$$|L(v)| \leq \left(\sum_{i=1}^{2000} L(e_i)^2\right)^{1/2} \|a\|_2 \leq \frac{\left(\sum L(e_i)^2\right)^{1/2}}{m}\,\|v\|.$$
Thus $L$ is bounded: there exists $C = \frac{(\sum L(e_i)^2)^{1/2}}{m}$ with $|L(v)| \leq C\|v\|$ for all $v \in V$.

Applying this with $\|\cdot\| = \|\cdot\|_1$ and $L(p) = p(0)$, we obtain a constant $C$ (depending only on the degree bound $1999$, not on $p$) such that
$$|p(0)| = |L(p)| \leq C\,\|p\|_1 = C\int_{-1}^{1}|p(x)|\,dx$$
for every polynomial $p$ of degree at most $1999$, in particular for every polynomial of degree exactly $1999$.

This completes the proof. $\blacksquare$

